# Reviewed gitleaks findings.
#
# Each line is a fingerprint: commit, file, rule and line. That specificity is
# the point — an entry here cannot mask a finding anywhere else, in any other
# commit, or even on a different line of the same file. A broad allowlist for
# the test directory would turn off the check that catches a real credential
# committed there later, which is the opposite of what a security scan is for.
#
# Every entry below is the same thing: a fabricated fixture value in
# scripts/tests/stage-scripts.test.sh, the test that proves the stage preflight
# and smoke runner never print a secret. The test needs values that look like
# credentials, because a fixture of "hunter2" would pass a leak assertion that
# a real-looking value fails. Nothing here was ever issued and nothing grants
# access to anything.
#
# gitleaks and GitGuardian were both right to flag them: a committed
# 32-character random-looking literal is indistinguishable from a real
# credential, and no scanner can know otherwise. So the literals are gone from
# the working tree — the fixtures are now generated per run from /dev/urandom,
# which also makes the leak assertions stronger, since each run searches for a
# string that never existed before.
#
# These entries exist only because the commit that introduced the literals is
# already published, and rewriting published history is not something to do for
# a false positive. No future commit can carry these values.

